---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const updated = 'September 28, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy | TwitterTools"
  description="TwitterTools privacy policy: what we collect (almost nothing), what we never collect (your X credentials), and how the tools work without accounts."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <div class="prose-none mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-slate-700">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">The short version</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          TwitterTools has no accounts, no sign-up and no login. The counting, parsing and search
          building tools run entirely in your browser — your text never leaves your device. For
          the downloader and thread reader we briefly fetch the public post you ask for. We never
          ask for your X (Twitter) credentials and we never post on your behalf.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">What we process</h2>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>Post links you submit</strong> to the downloader or thread reader. These are
            used to fetch the post from X's public embed endpoint and are cached for a few minutes
            to keep things fast. The cache is not used to build profiles and expires automatically.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Server logs.</strong> Our web server keeps standard access logs (requested
            URL, timestamp, user agent) for security and abuse prevention — for example to enforce
            the download rate limit. Logs are rotated and not shared.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Aggregate analytics.</strong> We use Cloudflare Web Analytics to count page
            views. It is cookie-free, does not fingerprint visitors and collects no personal
            data, so individual visitors cannot be identified — we only see which pages and tools
            are used most.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Everything else stays local.</strong> Character counting, URL/ID parsing and
            search query building happen in your browser via JavaScript. Nothing is transmitted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Local storage.</strong> The Bookmark Manager stores your saved posts, tags and
            notes in your browser's own IndexedDB. That data never leaves your device, is not
            backed up by us, and can be removed at any time from the Bookmark Manager (or by
            clearing site data in your browser).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Cookies & advertising</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          TwitterTools sets no cookies of its own — including our analytics, which works without
          them. If advertising is enabled in the future, the ad network (e.g. Google AdSense) may
          set its own cookies subject to its own policy; this section will be updated before that
          happens.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Third-party content</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          When you download media, files stream directly from X's media servers
          (pbs.twimg.com / video.twimg.com) through our proxy. We do not store media files. All
          content belongs to its original authors.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Your rights & contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Since we don't collect personal data or accounts, there is usually nothing to export or
          delete. If you have questions or a privacy request, contact
          <span class="font-mono text-sm">contact@twittertools.com</span>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
